WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.492 --> 00:00:06.950
JOE CORKERY: I'm
Joe Corkery, and I

00:00:06.950 --> 00:00:08.825
lead the Healthcare and
Life Sciences product

00:00:08.825 --> 00:00:10.520
team at Google Cloud.

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:12.332
When we first started
planning for Next,

00:00:12.332 --> 00:00:14.540
we intended for this talk
to be about how health care

00:00:14.540 --> 00:00:17.540
organizations are transforming
their businesses using

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.520
AI and ML.

00:00:19.520 --> 00:00:21.230
However, since that
time, the world--

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:23.210
and especially as it
relates to health care--

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:25.460
has seen a completely different
type of transformation

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:27.890
as a result of COVID-19.

00:00:27.890 --> 00:00:30.890
With that in mind, we decided
to focus this talk more

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:34.130
about how Google Cloud is
helping organizations respond

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:37.510
to COVID-19,
leveraging AI and ML,

00:00:37.510 --> 00:00:39.860
Google Cloud's Healthcare
and Life Sciences products,

00:00:39.860 --> 00:00:43.520
as well as some of the work
that our partners are doing.

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:46.070
I want to start by talking
through Google Cloud

00:00:46.070 --> 00:00:48.320
Healthcare and Life Sciences
team's mission, which

00:00:48.320 --> 00:00:50.870
is to create solutions to
empower provider, payer,

00:00:50.870 --> 00:00:53.750
and pharma organizations
to transform their business

00:00:53.750 --> 00:00:56.010
and the delivery of health care.

00:00:56.010 --> 00:00:58.280
Our development efforts
to deliver on that mission

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:01.130
are primarily organized
around four main pillars.

00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:04.640
Organize-- we want to make
it easy to ingest, normalize,

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:08.120
and join data to unlock
the potential of your data.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:12.020
Accessible-- we want to
support open standards, APIs,

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.350
interoperability and discovery,
to ensure that the data can

00:01:15.350 --> 00:01:17.650
be made accessible for use.

00:01:17.650 --> 00:01:21.540
Secure-- we lead the industry
in security, privacy,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:23.600
and compliance, from
endpoint to data center.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:26.000
Everything we do here
is core to our mission

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.270
and at the heart of our work.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:30.235
Useful-- with
secure, accessible,

00:01:30.235 --> 00:01:33.760
and well-organized data, we aim
to help organizations generate

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:36.280
insights and new capabilities
that advance the Quadruple

00:01:36.280 --> 00:01:37.920
Aim of health care.

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:40.180
The Quadruple Aim of
health care is a framework

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:44.140
of goals designed to improve
the patient experience of care,

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.450
improve the health
of populations,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.550
reduce the per capita
cost of health care,

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:51.860
and improve the work experience
of clinicians and staff.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:53.920
This useful pillar is
especially critical today

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:56.770
during the COVID19
pandemic, as organizations

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.770
seek to understand and take
action on a wide range of data

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:01.450
available to them.

00:02:01.450 --> 00:02:03.270
Here's our overall
agenda for the session,

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.935
which we've divided into
three distinct parts.

00:02:05.935 --> 00:02:08.310
In the first session, we will
cover the application of AI

00:02:08.310 --> 00:02:10.270
to COVID forecasting,
and we'll be

00:02:10.270 --> 00:02:13.060
joined by Dr. Thomas Tsai,
a surgeon and health policy

00:02:13.060 --> 00:02:15.280
researcher in the
Department of Surgery

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.920
at Brigham and Women's Hospital
and in the Department of Health

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.380
Policy and Management at
the Harvard T.H. Chan School

00:02:20.380 --> 00:02:21.815
of Public Health.

00:02:21.815 --> 00:02:24.190
He's affiliated with the
Harvard Global Health Institute,

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:25.648
with whom Google
Cloud is partnered

00:02:25.648 --> 00:02:29.310
on the development of the
COVID-19 public forecasts.

00:02:29.310 --> 00:02:31.270
In the second session,
we'll dive a bit deeper

00:02:31.270 --> 00:02:33.610
into how Google Cloud's
health care-specific product

00:02:33.610 --> 00:02:37.342
offerings are being used
to address COVID-19.

00:02:37.342 --> 00:02:38.800
Finally, in our
last session, we'll

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.260
highlight work being done
by one of our partners,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:44.860
Schrodinger, and how it's
being used to combat COVID-19.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:47.670
Let's get started.

00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.187
Now I'd like to welcome
Dr. Thomas Tsai to share

00:02:50.187 --> 00:02:52.020
his thoughts on our
collaboration to deliver

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.270
the COVID-19 public forecasts.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:56.820
THOMAS TSAI: Thank you,
Joe, for that introduction.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:59.350
As Joe said, my
name is Tom Tsai.

00:02:59.350 --> 00:03:01.650
I'm a surgeon and assistant
professor in health policy

00:03:01.650 --> 00:03:04.380
and management at the Harvard
School of Public Health

00:03:04.380 --> 00:03:06.660
and a faculty member of
the Harvard Global Health

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:08.070
Institute.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.170
The goal of the Harvard
Global Health Institute

00:03:10.170 --> 00:03:13.710
is to provide actionable
data to guide policymaking

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:15.240
to improve health
in both the United

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.760
States and the global context.

00:03:17.760 --> 00:03:20.910
During COVID-19, our efforts
have focused around three core

00:03:20.910 --> 00:03:21.970
areas--

00:03:21.970 --> 00:03:24.270
developing models of
US hospital capacity

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:26.670
to help plan for
storage capacity

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:30.180
to meet the demand of
COVID-19 cases, supporting

00:03:30.180 --> 00:03:34.000
federal and state policymakers
in developing, testing targets,

00:03:34.000 --> 00:03:37.920
and a contact tracing
strategy, and, three,

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.200
providing scientific
communication

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.460
to combat misinformation.

00:03:42.460 --> 00:03:44.970
We are public health
researchers, health policy

00:03:44.970 --> 00:03:46.920
experts and clinicians.

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:49.610
We try to bring all those
perspectives to bear

00:03:49.610 --> 00:03:52.830
when informing policy response
to urgent health care needs,

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:56.225
such as the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:03:56.225 --> 00:03:57.600
Over the last few
months, we have

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:01.410
embarked on a national
conversation in conjunction

00:04:01.410 --> 00:04:03.450
with the Harvard Safra
Center for Ethics

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:07.230
and multiple academic groups,
think tanks, foundations,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.450
and policy groups to
create convergence

00:04:09.450 --> 00:04:13.710
around a key set of metrics
for suppression of COVID-19.

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:17.190
This has resulted in our
COVID-19 risk level dashboard

00:04:17.190 --> 00:04:21.368
and test target dashboard,
globalepidemics.org.

00:04:21.368 --> 00:04:24.570
Our goal has been to not just
show the underlying risk,

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.760
but more importantly
tie the community level

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:29.880
risk to a corresponding
set of public health

00:04:29.880 --> 00:04:33.180
actions around testing
and non-pharmacological

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.230
interventions, such as social
distancing and even shutdowns.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:39.610
While the dashboard
shows us where we are,

00:04:39.610 --> 00:04:42.940
what it doesn't do is predict
what will happen in the future.

00:04:42.940 --> 00:04:44.940
And because of this,
states and even counties

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.760
are unable to fully prepare
for what is to come.

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.240
If hospitals policymakers,
and public health officials

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.600
could predict with more
reliability future COVID-19 19

00:04:54.600 --> 00:04:57.400
outbreaks, then they
could be better prepared.

00:04:57.400 --> 00:04:59.790
This is where artificial
intelligence and machine

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:01.680
learning come into play.

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:03.700
Through AI and
machine learning, we

00:05:03.700 --> 00:05:05.400
can create forecast
models that are

00:05:05.400 --> 00:05:08.850
able to predict cases, deaths,
and other important metrics

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:13.530
over the next 14 days, at both
the US state and county level.

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.950
And this is where
Google Cloud comes in.

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:20.680
Cloud AI, in partnership with
the Harvard Global Health

00:05:20.680 --> 00:05:23.920
Institute, has developed a
novel COVID-19 forecasting

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:27.190
model that is able to
predict cases, deaths,

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.100
and other important metrics
such as hospitalizations

00:05:30.100 --> 00:05:33.760
in the 14-day period at the
US state and county level.

00:05:33.760 --> 00:05:35.650
These forecasts are
powered by machine

00:05:35.650 --> 00:05:38.560
learning informed
epidemiological models that

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:42.990
continuously learn from
underlying public data sets.

00:05:42.990 --> 00:05:46.430
The forecasts are available
free to the public.

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:49.080
To generate the COVID-19
public forecasts,

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:51.960
Google Cloud researchers
developed a novel time series

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.300
machine learning
approach that combines

00:05:54.300 --> 00:05:57.360
artificial intelligence with
a robust epidemiological

00:05:57.360 --> 00:05:58.620
foundation.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:06:01.860
By design, this new model
is trained on public data

00:06:01.860 --> 00:06:04.710
and leverages an architecture
that allows researchers

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.580
to dive into the
different relationships

00:06:06.580 --> 00:06:09.000
that the model has learned
to better interpret

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:12.030
why it makes certain forecasts.

00:06:12.030 --> 00:06:15.930
One of the challenges of a
novel pandemic like COVID-19

00:06:15.930 --> 00:06:18.390
is that traditional
epidemiological modeling

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:21.300
depends on assumptions
of certain parameters,

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.470
such as the transmission
rate, the incubation period,

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:28.210
and the risk of mortality.

00:06:28.210 --> 00:06:31.560
What the AI machine
learning approach enables

00:06:31.560 --> 00:06:35.610
is an opportunity to learn
from the data in real time

00:06:35.610 --> 00:06:38.130
as you're progressing
through this pandemic.

00:06:38.130 --> 00:06:40.560
We hope that these
measures not only

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:43.710
help the public understand
how the model works, but more

00:06:43.710 --> 00:06:45.810
importantly, can enable
further innovations

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:47.743
in infectious disease modeling.

00:06:47.743 --> 00:06:49.410
We're calling on
public health officials

00:06:49.410 --> 00:06:52.380
to prioritize data
quality and transparency,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:55.440
as these underlying data are
what informs and enables the AI

00:06:55.440 --> 00:06:58.110
models to make accurate
predictions of health care

00:06:58.110 --> 00:06:59.460
professionals.

00:06:59.460 --> 00:07:01.620
As these data are
public, we hope

00:07:01.620 --> 00:07:03.420
users will use
this data to create

00:07:03.420 --> 00:07:06.180
their own dashboards
to guide and inform

00:07:06.180 --> 00:07:08.290
local decision making.

00:07:08.290 --> 00:07:10.410
There's a saying that
all models are wrong,

00:07:10.410 --> 00:07:12.790
but some models are useful.

00:07:12.790 --> 00:07:16.290
The COVID-19 public forecast
model is uniquely positioned

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.930
to provide not just
useful information,

00:07:18.930 --> 00:07:21.720
but more importantly,
actionable insights

00:07:21.720 --> 00:07:26.067
that can be used to help
suppress the COVID-19 pandemic.

00:07:26.067 --> 00:07:28.650
It's been our privilege at the
Harvard Global Health Institute

00:07:28.650 --> 00:07:32.430
to partner with Google Cloud
on this very important effort.

00:07:32.430 --> 00:07:36.298
We look forward to continue
this ongoing collaboration.

00:07:36.298 --> 00:07:37.590
JOE CORKERY: Thank, you Thomas.

00:07:37.590 --> 00:07:40.970
I really appreciate you
participating in this session.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.370
The COVID-19 public
forecast model

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:47.240
generates a 14-day forecast
of the development of COVID-19

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:49.460
in each US state and county.

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:51.440
It's intended to be used
as one of many signals

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:54.550
to help first responders in
health care, the public sector,

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:57.560
and other impacted organizations
to be better prepared for what

00:07:57.560 --> 00:07:59.330
lies ahead.

00:07:59.330 --> 00:08:00.830
The data is available
in BigQuery,

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:03.695
where users can interact with
it directly and without cost,

00:08:03.695 --> 00:08:06.650
as it is included in the
COVID-19 public data set

00:08:06.650 --> 00:08:09.580
program that makes a large
number of COVID-related data

00:08:09.580 --> 00:08:12.270
sets freely searchable
on BigQuery.

00:08:12.270 --> 00:08:15.110
the data can also be
downloaded directly as a CSV

00:08:15.110 --> 00:08:18.020
for your own analysis
and your own tools.

00:08:18.020 --> 00:08:20.840
Finally, we also created a
high level dashboard view

00:08:20.840 --> 00:08:23.180
of the data using Google
Data Studio, which

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:24.940
is what you're seeing here.

00:08:24.940 --> 00:08:26.690
Let's take a closer
look at that dashboard

00:08:26.690 --> 00:08:30.600
now to get a better
understanding of the results.

00:08:30.600 --> 00:08:34.330
In the COVID-19 public
forecast data studio dashboard,

00:08:34.330 --> 00:08:36.809
you will see data projections
over the next 14 days

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:38.370
for the United States.

00:08:38.370 --> 00:08:40.530
The forecasts are
generated roughly every day

00:08:40.530 --> 00:08:43.360
and are available for all
US states and counties.

00:08:43.360 --> 00:08:45.420
This dashboard shows
historical data side

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:47.947
by side with these forecasts.

00:08:47.947 --> 00:08:50.280
The map view allows you to
see which states are expected

00:08:50.280 --> 00:08:52.470
to have the highest increase
in the number of deaths

00:08:52.470 --> 00:08:55.100
and confirmed cases.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.500
You can also quickly
navigate between states

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.850
to view the forecast
for each one.

00:08:59.850 --> 00:09:01.800
Let's pick Arizona.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:04.740
Additional metrics such
as hospitalizations, ICU,

00:09:04.740 --> 00:09:07.680
and ventilator forecasts
are also available.

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:10.110
For US counties, historical
and forecast data

00:09:10.110 --> 00:09:13.980
is available for cases,
deaths, and hospitalizations.

00:09:13.980 --> 00:09:16.620
You can use the dropdown menu
to select one or more counties

00:09:16.620 --> 00:09:19.623
and view a time series
visualization for that county.

00:09:19.623 --> 00:09:23.250
Let's pick Yuma.

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.230
Additional data at the
county and state level

00:09:25.230 --> 00:09:29.708
is available in the
BigQuery table and CSVs.

00:09:29.708 --> 00:09:32.250
In addition to our partnership
with the Harvard Global Health

00:09:32.250 --> 00:09:34.290
Institute, we work
closely with SADA

00:09:34.290 --> 00:09:38.010
and HCA Healthcare care to make
the COVID-19 public forecasts

00:09:38.010 --> 00:09:40.260
available in the
national response portal,

00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:42.600
with the goal of ensuring as
many people can have access

00:09:42.600 --> 00:09:44.510
to the data as possible.

00:09:44.510 --> 00:09:47.510
Many organizations have found
value from these types of data,

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:52.520
including HCA, as highlighted
here by Dr. Edmond Jackson.

00:09:52.520 --> 00:09:54.500
Our hope is that other
organizations will also

00:09:54.500 --> 00:09:57.470
be able to take this data and
use it to plan accordingly

00:09:57.470 --> 00:10:00.680
to address COVID-19 needs
at the hospital level.

00:10:00.680 --> 00:10:03.590
Let's change gears now to focus
on Google Cloud's health care

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:05.450
specific products
and how they're

00:10:05.450 --> 00:10:08.950
being used to help with
the COVID-19 crisis.

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:10.650
We just talked about
how organizations

00:10:10.650 --> 00:10:13.530
like HCA Healthcare are
benefiting from AI/ML

00:10:13.530 --> 00:10:15.900
from COVID forecasting.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:18.437
Data is what powers
these ML models.

00:10:18.437 --> 00:10:20.520
However, the data plumbing
required in health care

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:24.010
to build those models is
challenging and time consuming.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:26.070
The Google Cloud health
care API is designed

00:10:26.070 --> 00:10:29.790
to make the data plumbing
easier, cheaper, and faster

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:33.030
to accelerate health data
interoperability and enable you

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.260
to unlock the power of your
data to create and/or consume

00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:40.400
AI models, to run large-scale
cross-modality analytics,

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:43.680
and to build and deploy
novel health applications.

00:10:43.680 --> 00:10:45.210
Let's continue with
the COVID theme

00:10:45.210 --> 00:10:47.910
and walk through an example of
how a large provider system was

00:10:47.910 --> 00:10:50.940
able to quickly gain insights
into COVID admissions using

00:10:50.940 --> 00:10:52.800
the health care API.

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:54.870
When COVID hit, it
exposed a real problem

00:10:54.870 --> 00:10:56.760
in many provider systems.

00:10:56.760 --> 00:10:58.920
The disease was moving
faster than the data

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:01.500
that they were using to
make their decisions.

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:03.060
Some of the
challenges they faced

00:11:03.060 --> 00:11:07.500
included data latency, where
their existing data analytics

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:10.290
ran off Epic Clarity
exports, which can often

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:12.690
be more than 24 hours old.

00:11:12.690 --> 00:11:16.200
Data continuity-- regional
hospitals and clinics each

00:11:16.200 --> 00:11:19.680
had their own siloed view
of just their own data.

00:11:19.680 --> 00:11:22.130
And finally,
available IT skills--

00:11:22.130 --> 00:11:24.380
a real lack of
local IT staff made

00:11:24.380 --> 00:11:27.740
rollout of a new technical
solution impossible.

00:11:27.740 --> 00:11:31.000
What they needed was a way
to tap into real-time events,

00:11:31.000 --> 00:11:34.400
and in particular those that are
broadcast on the HL7v2 message

00:11:34.400 --> 00:11:35.760
stream.

00:11:35.760 --> 00:11:38.130
The solution that they
developed leverage the real time

00:11:38.130 --> 00:11:41.880
feed by consuming the
HL7v2 message feed directly

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:45.090
into the health care API,
transforming that data

00:11:45.090 --> 00:11:47.490
into FHIR, and then
projecting that data

00:11:47.490 --> 00:11:50.070
into BigQuery for analytics.

00:11:50.070 --> 00:11:51.620
The data feeds
were then combined

00:11:51.620 --> 00:11:53.360
across regional
hospitals and clinics

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:57.480
to create a single aggregated
data set for the entire system.

00:11:57.480 --> 00:12:00.220
They were then able to use
pre-built Looker dashboards

00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:03.340
that combine national data
with their local data,

00:12:03.340 --> 00:12:06.440
as well as their own data,
to gain better insights.

00:12:06.440 --> 00:12:09.620
Let's dive a bit deeper into
the specifics of the solution.

00:12:09.620 --> 00:12:11.620
The Google Cloud
health care API was

00:12:11.620 --> 00:12:15.730
the key to moving from sometime
to real-time analytics.

00:12:15.730 --> 00:12:19.000
We were able to plug into the
Rhapsody interface engine,

00:12:19.000 --> 00:12:21.850
and land the HL7 messages
directly into the health care

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:23.860
API message store.

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:26.290
We then leveraged our
data harmonization suite

00:12:26.290 --> 00:12:30.370
to transform the HL7v2 messages
into a FHIR representation

00:12:30.370 --> 00:12:31.776
in real time.

00:12:31.776 --> 00:12:34.340
The health care API FHIR
store can automatically

00:12:34.340 --> 00:12:36.490
project the data
through to BigQuery

00:12:36.490 --> 00:12:39.590
and can be visualized
using a Looker dashboard.

00:12:39.590 --> 00:12:41.450
Traditionally,
standing up this kind

00:12:41.450 --> 00:12:44.550
of a solution within the health
care system would take months,

00:12:44.550 --> 00:12:47.010
but we needed to act
at the speed of COVID.

00:12:47.010 --> 00:12:50.030
And by using the health care
API, we were able to do this.

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:52.430
The result was that the
C-suite had visibility

00:12:52.430 --> 00:12:56.390
into real-time data about the
number of cases, resources,

00:12:56.390 --> 00:12:57.543
and utilization.

00:12:57.543 --> 00:12:59.210
And all of this was
delivered from start

00:12:59.210 --> 00:13:01.490
to finish in just three weeks.

00:13:01.490 --> 00:13:04.100
Before we move on, I want to
take a slightly closer look

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:06.682
at the architecture
behind the solution.

00:13:06.682 --> 00:13:08.390
The first step in
delivering the solution

00:13:08.390 --> 00:13:10.190
was setting up the
cloud environment

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:12.650
using the data
protection toolkit, which

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:15.800
automates the deployment of
cloud projects and resources

00:13:15.800 --> 00:13:19.580
following HIPAA best practices,
automating permissions

00:13:19.580 --> 00:13:21.800
using the principle
of least privilege,

00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:23.970
and setting up log retention.

00:13:23.970 --> 00:13:27.210
Once patients are admitted,
transferred, or discharged,

00:13:27.210 --> 00:13:29.780
the EHR, or the
electronic health record,

00:13:29.780 --> 00:13:33.290
sends out an HL7 message, which
our Pub/Sub adapter listens

00:13:33.290 --> 00:13:37.730
for, captures, and delivers
to the health care API HL7v2

00:13:37.730 --> 00:13:39.292
message store.

00:13:39.292 --> 00:13:42.590
The HL7v2 data was
converted to FHIR

00:13:42.590 --> 00:13:46.610
via transformation pipeline
run in Cloud Data Fusion.

00:13:46.610 --> 00:13:48.110
However, that wasn't
the only source

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:50.310
of data brought
into the solution.

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:52.530
We also brought in data
from operational systems

00:13:52.530 --> 00:13:53.895
in CSV format.

00:13:53.895 --> 00:13:56.750
We worked with the system to
create a Whistle Mapping config

00:13:56.750 --> 00:14:00.330
to harmonize the data into FHIR
from these different sources,

00:14:00.330 --> 00:14:03.800
which were in different schemas,
terminologies, and value sets.

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:05.990
As a result, both
operational and clinical data

00:14:05.990 --> 00:14:09.020
are harmonized in real time
to the FHIR store, which

00:14:09.020 --> 00:14:11.780
then streams data directly into
BigQuery so that the Looker

00:14:11.780 --> 00:14:13.760
dashboards reflect up
to date information

00:14:13.760 --> 00:14:15.830
for decision-making.

00:14:15.830 --> 00:14:17.498
As we discussed on
the previous slide,

00:14:17.498 --> 00:14:19.790
there's a lot of value in
the structured data contained

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:23.337
in the HL7 messages and
the operational data.

00:14:23.337 --> 00:14:24.920
However, there is a
significant amount

00:14:24.920 --> 00:14:27.260
of value locked up in
unstructured data contained

00:14:27.260 --> 00:14:30.080
in the clinical narrative
of the progress notes.

00:14:30.080 --> 00:14:33.905
Extracting this value
requires a different approach.

00:14:33.905 --> 00:14:36.030
Let's dive a little bit
deeper into clinical entity

00:14:36.030 --> 00:14:37.480
extraction.

00:14:37.480 --> 00:14:39.180
What you're seeing
here is a small part

00:14:39.180 --> 00:14:41.160
of a typical ICU note.

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:42.390
It's very dense.

00:14:42.390 --> 00:14:44.880
There are lots of
unintuitive abbreviations.

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:47.070
There's weird punctuation,
because large sections

00:14:47.070 --> 00:14:49.640
are often generated by the EHR.

00:14:49.640 --> 00:14:53.290
And there are two languages
here, both English and Latin.

00:14:53.290 --> 00:14:55.750
But there is often more
detail and richness of context

00:14:55.750 --> 00:14:57.235
buried in the clinical note.

00:14:57.235 --> 00:14:58.790
And that is important
to understand,

00:14:58.790 --> 00:15:01.820
and it is not available in
the structured EHR data.

00:15:01.820 --> 00:15:03.770
So what can we do about this?

00:15:03.770 --> 00:15:05.470
The Healthcare
Natural Language API,

00:15:05.470 --> 00:15:07.450
which is coming
soon in beta, will

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:11.260
help you find, assess, and link
the knowledge in your data.

00:15:11.260 --> 00:15:14.080
In particular, it allows you
to extract clinically relevant

00:15:14.080 --> 00:15:16.390
entities from a
text to understand

00:15:16.390 --> 00:15:18.850
the relationship
between those entities

00:15:18.850 --> 00:15:21.370
to understand the context in
which those entities appear

00:15:21.370 --> 00:15:23.560
in the text, as
well as to associate

00:15:23.560 --> 00:15:25.960
those with known ontologies.

00:15:25.960 --> 00:15:28.510
In addition to the capabilities
offered by the Healthcare

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.710
Natural Language API, the
need for custom information

00:15:31.710 --> 00:15:34.530
extraction models that have
high fidelity for health care

00:15:34.530 --> 00:15:37.700
and life sciences
applications is significant.

00:15:37.700 --> 00:15:39.810
AutoML entity extraction
for health care,

00:15:39.810 --> 00:15:41.820
which is also coming
soon, provides you

00:15:41.820 --> 00:15:44.370
with the ability to
generate high quality custom

00:15:44.370 --> 00:15:48.750
models for health applications
with no coding skills required.

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:51.040
Just upload and label
the training data,

00:15:51.040 --> 00:15:53.010
train the model with
the simple click,

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:55.810
and then deploy
that model at scale.

00:15:55.810 --> 00:15:57.880
A major US health center
was seeing patients

00:15:57.880 --> 00:16:01.830
presenting to them with a wide
variety of different symptoms

00:16:01.830 --> 00:16:04.330
through multiple clinical entry
points, including their call

00:16:04.330 --> 00:16:07.450
centers, chatbots, telemedicine
visits, and of course

00:16:07.450 --> 00:16:09.890
in-person care.

00:16:09.890 --> 00:16:11.330
The data that was
being collected

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:14.560
at the different entry points
was not connected together,

00:16:14.560 --> 00:16:17.060
and there was not an easy way
to pull all this data together

00:16:17.060 --> 00:16:20.860
to identify common patterns
in their population.

00:16:20.860 --> 00:16:22.520
In response, they
decided to kick off

00:16:22.520 --> 00:16:24.440
a pilot to automate
the extraction

00:16:24.440 --> 00:16:28.460
of clinical information from
these unstructured data sources

00:16:28.460 --> 00:16:31.220
to better make more accurate
predictions and better

00:16:31.220 --> 00:16:33.710
triage their COVID-19 patients.

00:16:33.710 --> 00:16:36.330
The early results of this
effort were promising.

00:16:36.330 --> 00:16:38.330
They pooled all the
disparate data sets together

00:16:38.330 --> 00:16:41.150
into a single store and
also enhanced their chatbots

00:16:41.150 --> 00:16:44.420
to ask COVID-19 specific
questions as well.

00:16:44.420 --> 00:16:46.970
As a result of their ability
to better extract information

00:16:46.970 --> 00:16:48.840
from multiple
sources, they've seen

00:16:48.840 --> 00:16:51.660
many improvements in the
accuracy of their diagnoses,

00:16:51.660 --> 00:16:54.265
as well as a reduction
in their time to treat.

00:16:54.265 --> 00:16:56.640
They're also exploring how
this can be used in the future

00:16:56.640 --> 00:17:01.040
to better identify chronic
conditions related to COVID.

00:17:01.040 --> 00:17:04.510
To close, I want to
highlight one of our partners

00:17:04.510 --> 00:17:08.839
that's working on finding
new treatments for COVID-19.

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:10.910
Schrodinger is one
of those partners.

00:17:10.910 --> 00:17:13.990
Schrodinger is a drug and
material discovery company

00:17:13.990 --> 00:17:17.230
that has built a platform that
uses physics and chemistry

00:17:17.230 --> 00:17:20.170
principles to create
computational models that

00:17:20.170 --> 00:17:22.720
predict how different
substances will interact

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:24.650
at the molecular level.

00:17:24.650 --> 00:17:27.810
These simulations can be
run at an enormous scale

00:17:27.810 --> 00:17:30.580
to assess billions of
molecules in silico

00:17:30.580 --> 00:17:34.050
before selecting candidates
to take into the lab.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.210
We announced our initial
partnership in May

00:17:36.210 --> 00:17:38.850
when Google Cloud was providing
Schrodinger with access

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:42.090
to powerful computing capacity
to accelerate drug discovery

00:17:42.090 --> 00:17:45.750
for its commercial partners
and its own internal pipeline.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:48.240
However, more recently, and
in response to the COVID-19

00:17:48.240 --> 00:17:50.760
pandemic, Google Cloud
is providing Schrodinger

00:17:50.760 --> 00:17:53.250
with 16 million
hours of GPU time

00:17:53.250 --> 00:17:55.610
through our researcher
credit program.

00:17:55.610 --> 00:17:57.900
And this is to enable
computational drug discovery

00:17:57.900 --> 00:18:00.870
for COVID-19, which
if used consecutively,

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:06.230
would equate to 1,826 years
of around the clock computing.

00:18:06.230 --> 00:18:08.010
To accelerate this
drug discovery process,

00:18:08.010 --> 00:18:11.360
Schrodinger has teamed up
with Takeda, Novartis, Gilead

00:18:11.360 --> 00:18:14.810
Sciences, and WuXi AppTech
in a philanthropic initiative

00:18:14.810 --> 00:18:17.720
to share ideas,
resources, and data,

00:18:17.720 --> 00:18:20.420
with the goal of developing
novel and viral therapeutics

00:18:20.420 --> 00:18:22.227
for COVID-19.

00:18:22.227 --> 00:18:24.560
We're very excited to be able
to support this initiative

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.642
and we wish them the
very best of luck.

00:18:26.642 --> 00:18:28.850
We hope that you've found
this useful and informative

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:31.160
and that this will be
helpful for your organization

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.710
as you combat the
COVID-19 crisis.

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:35.623
We're now in the
final month of Next.

00:18:35.623 --> 00:18:37.790
We have several Healthcare
and Life Science sessions

00:18:37.790 --> 00:18:39.412
that you might find useful.

00:18:39.412 --> 00:18:41.120
You can easily find
this information link

00:18:41.120 --> 00:18:43.880
from the Google Cloud Healthcare
and Life Sciences website,

00:18:43.880 --> 00:18:46.230
or from our Next home page.

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:48.100
Thank you very much.

